up|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|West played the hand well. Now I have a couple of points to discuss. First, if I didn't bid 4@C, West should bid 3N. @CAx is not a great stopper. But partner of preemptor rates to have at most 2 clubs, so as long as preemptor has no side entry, 3N makes. Or at least rates to make.|
PG| ***** 17 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|However, after I bid 4@C, it gets more difficult, which is why you should strain to follow |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=lott,Law of Total Tricks^*N|CP||FP||at| when competing.|

n#|9|at|^^^fIf you do follow the link you can return  HERE  by clicking the  |fp|1|n#|3|at|@L|fp||n^|3|at|   icon button, top left.|
PG| ***** 18 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In that case, I think double is the best choice. And that is not takeout per se. It is, as suggested ^*BDo Something Intelligent^*n or value showing.|
PG| ***** 19 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Lots of times partner will pass with a trick or two and a reasonably balanced hand. Over double, East, with an unbalanced hand should bid. And, although the diamonds are much better than the spades, I would bid 4@S because it has more upside in the sense that 4@D is not game and 4@S is game.|
PG| ***** 20 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Again, this illustrates why you preempt because it forces the opponents to guess as to what to do. Despite the bad break, 4@S makes 5 losing only one heart and @SA.|
PG| ***** 21 ***** |


