mb|P|mb|1S|mb|P|mb|2D|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Playing 2/1 game forcing, 2@D (which is forcing to game) is the correct bid. Basically, the way you determine whether or not you have an opening hand, even if you don't have a fit is that if your hand meets the |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=ruleof20,Rule of 20^*N|CP||FP||at|, consider it a game forcing hand.|
PG| ***** 42 ***** |


mb|P|mb|2H|mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|?|lb|*mWhich one is your pick for East? Click your choice.^3H~4H~I have no clue~Let me Google it^1|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Let's discuss 3@H vs 4@H with East hand. If East thought the hand was a minimum game force, e.g barely an opener East would bid 4@H which is known as ^*BFast Arrival^*n. Since 2@D established a game force there is no reason to bid 4@H unless you have no slam interest, as 3@H can't be passed.|
PG| ***** 44 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|East has 11 HCP, 1 quality suit point, 3 length points for a 7 card suit, and 3 points for a singleton with 4 trump. I wouldn't count both singletons as counting one is fine. That is worth around 18 |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=dummypoints,Dummy Points^*N|CP||FP||at|.|
PG| ***** 45 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The only minuses are short spades (although the @SA is nice) and weak hearts. Therefore, I think 3@H is clearly much better than 4@H.|
PG| ***** 46 ***** |


mb|3H|mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|3S|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Once East bids 3@H, unless West has a really a minimum hand West should start control bidding. 3@S here is not showing a spade suit, it is specifically showing a spade control (A or K, or had West not bid spades already possibly singleton or void).|
PG| ***** 47 ***** |


mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|4C|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Here East is showing 2nd round club control (singleton in this case, although more often A or K).|
PG| ***** 48 ***** |


mb|D|an|lead directing|
pg| ***** 49 ***** |


mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|P|mb|4D|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|First, West could not bid 4@D as a control bid because if partner bids a suit naturally as 2@D by East, you only control bid aces or kings, not shortness. But my double gave them another chance for East to control bid 4@D.|
PG| ***** 50 ***** |


 
mb|P|mb|4H|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|P|mb|P|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Personally I would have continued trying for slam, although because of the bad heart break, slam is very difficult.|
PG| ***** 51 ***** |


