qx|A4|st||rh||ah|Board 4 IMPs   -    March 13, 2024|pn|hondo717,IraCT,danpsych,Toasterln|
md|2SQ93HT53DKQ9875C8,SAKJ4HAK982D6CK43,S62HQJ74DJ43CQJ96,ST875H6DAT2CAT752|sv|b|
mb|1H|mb|P|mb|1S|mb|P|mb|4D|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|4@D by West is a |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=splinters,Splinter^*N|CP||FP||at|. Since East can have as little as 6 points, West is showing 4 spades, a singleton diamond, and 19+ points.|
PG| ***** 68 ***** |


mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|4S|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I think 4@S is correct by East. On the positive side, East has the ideal diamond holding opposite a singleton (either 3+ small or 3+ with the Ace). However, the singleton heart given the 1@H opener and 4 little spades are problematical. The very likely 8-card fit means it is likely there are one or two spade losers.|
PG| ***** 69 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|East should know it is an 8-card fit because, unless West has 6 hearts and 5 spades, very unlikely, West could not possibly have 5 spades, because with 5-5 in the majors, West would open 1@S not 1@H.|
PG| ***** 70 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Keep that in mind in general. If partner opens a suit and then bids a major they almost always have exactly 4 cards in that major.|
PG| ***** 71 ***** |


mb|P?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BWest: ^*NI have a great hand and am thinking what should I bid.|
PG| ***** 72 ***** |


cp|8|AT|
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|It is not really a thinking hand. Partner rejected your |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=splinters,Splinter^*N|CP||FP||at| because partner has lots of wasted diamond points. So although it is barely possible you have slam you really don't have much more than you already promised. |
PG| ***** 73 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|If you couldn't open 2@C, then it is unlikely you have slam opposite partner. With 18 HCP, 1 quality suit point, 1 length point, 2 points for singleton, and 1 point for side heart suit you have 23 points and prime values.|
PG| ***** 74 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|That means partner needs 10 at least and no wasted diamond points. Yes, slam is possible but the general rule is ^*BGame before Slam^*n meaning unless there is a reasonable expectation that you have slam you should stop in game rather than trying for a marginal slam.|
PG| ***** 75 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BWest: ^*NIf I bid 5 clubs it would promise the ace.|
PG| ***** 76 ***** |


cp|8|AT|
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Yes, 5@C over 4@S shows the @CA. So your only choices to try for slam are 4NT or 5@H. So pass now is the most reasonable choice.|
PG| ***** 77 ***** |


mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|P|
pg| ***** 78 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I am not sure what the best lead is. normally a trump lead is a good idea when there is a splinter but with qxx I might be giving away a trick. @DK or club could both be right. On the other hand, in double dummy spade is probably best.|
PG| ***** 79 ***** |


