up|52|nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BEast: ^*NOver partner's 4@D, if I bid 4@H it would NOT be a singleton, correct?|
PG| ***** 93 ***** |


cp|8|AT|
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|That is correct. It would be a control bid, showing slam interest. However, since West opened hearts, you only control bid Aces and Kings in suits partner has bid not singletons and voids as they are not usually useful and often a detriment.|
PG| ***** 94 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|We have a lot of tough contracts and great declarer play tonight.|
PG| ***** 95 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Another way to play it would be to either draw 2 rounds of trump and set up clubs, or, if you were not afraid of a 4-1 spade break, take the spade hook, and when it works, draw 3 rounds of trump to set up clubs.|
PG| ***** 96 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|If you did that, you would take 4 spades, 1 ruff, 2 hearts, 1 diamond, and 4 clubs for 12 tricks. However, the way East played it was very safe and very reasonable.|
PG| ***** 97 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BEast: ^*NHow do I make that plan from the start?|
PG| ***** 98 ***** |


cp|8|AT|
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In this contract you should be worried about a club ruff. If you weren't or you were pretty sure the spade finesse worked you do have a play for 12 tricks. Still I liked your line of play.|
PG| ***** 99 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BEast: ^*NFor me, making a plan for Declarer play is the hardest part of the game.|
PG| ***** 100 ***** |


cp|8|AT|
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|It is very difficult and many don't know how to do it. I do have some declarer play lessons that might help on my web site. It takes lots of practice.|
PG| ***** 101 ***** |


