Hand 5
https://tinyurl.com/22y4ynzn
qx|A5|st||rh||ah|Board 5 IMPs   -    March 13, 2024|pn|hondo717,IraCT,Toasterln,danpsych|
md|3SAQTHJ7DQ9CKJT853,SJ65HT942DA84CQ42,S98732HAK6DJT7C97,SK4HQ853DK6532CA6|sv|n|
mb|P|mb|1D|mb|2C|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|2@C over 1@D is one of my favorite bids because it is somewhat preemptive and makes it hard for the opponents to find a major suit fit. So, over 1@D bid I strain to bid 2@C even with a marginal 2C overcall. However, here I have a clear 2@C overcall. No strain required.|
PG| ***** 103 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In fact, given partner passed, 3@C would not be out of the question. But with flat distribution and being vulnerable I think 3@C is a bit too aggressive.|
PG| ***** 104 ***** |


mb|P|mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|P|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I agree with the West pass and I think pass by East is reasonable, but notice they missed their heart fit.|
pg| ***** 105 ***** |


pc|DA|sk|s|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|pc|D7|pc|D6|pc|D9|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In general I don't like leading unprotected aces. However, if partner bid the suit, it often works out okay. Otherwise, it is definitely something you should avoid.|
pg| ***** 106 ***** |


